From 7cb15b22c38575cbeaf03483af0033c128332620 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: baKon <mike.ski83@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 17:55:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] engine: a collective with no device kernel for a message size
 refuses at startup

--tp 3 --tp-wire wht6 declares the lossy all-reduce beside the exact one, and no row implements it
at three ranks. Declare reported that as "host-only ops: all_reduce ... Not a hole" because the host
rows resolve at any world size, the server booted and answered /health, and the first prefill above
--tp-wire-min-kb stopped the process inside rad_issue (#46). A collective carries no weights, so
bind_ops keeps it on the device and the issue path never switches domain per band: a host kernel
does not cover it.

On a device backend a collective's device miss is now a hole in the miss report and refuses the
configuration at declare, naming the band and, when the wire is lossy, --tp-wire exact. Every layer
issues one over the step's residual stream and its bands run up to the largest step, so the first
chunk that selects it always lands there. The host backend (CPU-only serving) is untouched. Which
world sizes a wire covers stays the kernel libraries' answer: this reads what declare resolved.

The guide already said --tp 8 --tp-wire wht6 stops at startup; it now does, and so does --tp 3.
---
